WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.660
FedRAMP High has direct engineering implications.

00:00:02.660 --> 00:00:06.927
Strong identity and access control, privileged access management, least privilege,

00:00:06.927 --> 00:00:10.340
segmentation, encryption, secure configuration, vulnerability management, change control,

00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:14.607
audit logging, monitoring, incident response readiness, backup and recovery, resilience,

00:00:14.607 --> 00:00:16.313
secure development practices, infrastructure-as-code,

00:00:16.313 --> 00:00:20.580
and configuration baselines all become part of the operating picture.

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:23.567
High-impact services should be secure, observable, recoverable,

00:00:23.567 --> 00:00:25.273
and evidence-producing by design.

00:00:25.273 --> 00:00:31.247
Secure means controls are built into architecture and workflows rather than patched on later.

00:00:31.247 --> 00:00:35.087
Observable means security-relevant events can be collected, protected, reviewed,

00:00:35.087 --> 00:00:36.793
and used for investigation.

00:00:36.793 --> 00:00:42.767
Recoverable means the service can respond to disruption in ways that match mission expectations.

00:00:42.767 --> 00:00:47.460
Evidence-producing means normal operations generate records that support assessment and monitoring.

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:50.873
Engineering teams should reduce manual ambiguity wherever possible.

00:00:50.873 --> 00:00:55.140
Infrastructure-as-code can help enforce consistent environments and preserve change history.

00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:57.273
Configuration baselines can reduce drift.

00:00:57.273 --> 00:01:01.113
Centralized logging and monitoring can support detection and investigation.

00:01:01.113 --> 00:01:04.527
Automated vulnerability workflows can make remediation more visible.

00:01:04.527 --> 00:01:08.793
Access reviews, approval paths, and administrative logging can support accountability.

00:01:08.793 --> 00:01:13.060
These practices help the service sustain High expectations over time.

00:01:13.060 --> 00:01:14.767
High also requires maintainability.

00:01:14.767 --> 00:01:20.313
A control that depends on one specialist remembering an undocumented procedure is fragile.

00:01:20.313 --> 00:01:24.580
A control built into deployment pipelines, access systems, monitoring dashboards,

00:01:24.580 --> 00:01:27.993
ticketing workflows, and incident processes is more resilient.

00:01:27.993 --> 00:01:33.113
The engineering goal is not simply to produce evidence for an assessor;

00:01:33.113 --> 00:01:39.940
it is to operate a service whose security posture can be understood and maintained under pressure.
